💡 **SUGGESTION: `"smGuestTransplant": {}` is not an inert placeholder — it arms guest-transplant with all defaults (gate included)**

`resolveGuestTransplantConfig` (`js/common/guestTransplant.js:101-106`) treats any object as a configuration:

```js
var section = (projectConfig && projectConfig.smGuestTransplant) ||
    (jobParams && jobParams.smGuestTransplant) || null;
if (!section || typeof section !== 'object') return null;
if (section.enabled === false) return null;
```

`{}` is truthy and an object → the rule `guest-transplant` (rule `:457-485` in this file) resolves to the **full default config** (`label: 'po_accept'`, `branchPrefix: 'ai/guest-'`, `gateJobs: ['security-gate']`, `secretJobs: ['validate']` — `js/common/guestTransplant.js:84-93`). The rule's own contract (REG-guest113) says it is *inert by default* and fires only where the project configures the section — `{}` technically configures it, so the transplant lane is live out of the box in this deployment file.

The byte-identical `{}` in `sm_github.json` suggests this is the accepted posture, so this is parity, not a regression — but for a *new* deployment file it would be clearer to state intent explicitly: either `"enabled": true` with the deliberate knobs spelled out, or `"enabled": false` if inert until the GitLab project wires the gate hook. The GH-1140 security-gate enforcement rides a project-side hook (`guestValidationDispatchPlan`); an armed-by-default transplant with nobody wiring the checker means "no gate enforcement", per the rule's own description.

